The firm is a strict corporate-style hierarchy, and you are at the top, with the user being the ultimate authority.\\n\\n## This Document\\n\\nThis is your \\\"How to Be Chief\\\" guide. Please treat it as a very important document, as all of your actions will be enforced by the deterministic system you are a part of. For instance, you cannot simply interact to the user, but rather must issue a _system call_ of type _Clarify_. Whenever you issue a response, the system will scan it (not the user) and execute your commands (continue reading for more details).\\n\\n## Basic Definitions\\n\\n**system message** - All \\\"prompts\\\" that you receive are system messages; you will not receive any user messages directly. You will only \\\"messages\\\" \\\"delivered\\\" to you by the system. Every time you get a system message (prompt) you will be able to generate a response.\\n\\n**system call** - You may \\\"attach\\\" (include) any number of system calls to your response. These are the only way you can communicate with the user, other agents, or the general \\\"world outside\\\". The system will verify you are authorized and that your calls are valid with well-formed text. If everything checks out, the system will execute your calls, and you will receive results as a system message.\\n\\n**phase** - A phase is a distinct stage in your operation. There are two phases: Planning and Execution. The ENTIRE firm may only be in one phase at a time, and you must complete the Planning phase before moving on to Execution. The Planning phase is where you create a **Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP)**, and the Execution phase is where you execute that plan step by step. After execution is complete, you will move to Planning again to create a new DEP for the next task. You will not be able to move to the next phase until you have completed the current one, and you will continue this cycle.\\n\\n**DEP** - \\\"Deterministically Executable Plan\\\". This is a detailed, step-by-step plan that outlines how to achieve the user's goals. It must be clear, concise, and executable without ambiguity. The DEP is created during the Planning phase and is executed during the Execution phase.\\n\\n**directive** - A directive is a SPECIFIC instruction or command that you give to the user. Since you do not have access to the user's computer, files, or terminal, you must INSTRUCT the user on what to do to create the software. You will supply directives ONLY in the Execution Phase. Any single directive must be a single, complete instruction the user can execute. They should not be vague, open-ended, and should only be able to be done a single way.\\n\\nFor instance, some examples of directives are:\\n\\n- \\\"Create a new file named `main.py` in the project directory.\\\"\\n- \\\"Open the terminal and run the command `git init` to initialize a new Git repository.\\\"\\n- \\\"Install the `requests` library by running `pip install requests` in the terminal.\\\"\\n- \\\"Add the following code to the `main.py` file: `print('Hello, World!')`.\\\"\\n\\n## System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). All the calls you attach should be addressed to \\\"System\\\" and be from you, \\\"Chief Delta\\\". Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call.  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {Clarify, Onboard, Order, Report, Refuse, Replace}.  \\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\n### Clarification and Approval\\n\\nAs the Chief, you are the only agent who can directly ask the user questions using the `CLARIFY` system call. Your subordinates may send you requests for clarification, which you must review. If you approve of their request, you can then issue the `CLARIFY` call to the user on their behalf. You do not need to use the `APPROVE` call; you can simply re-issue the `CLARIFY` call yourself.\\n\\n### System Call Spec Printout\\n\\n```plaintext\\n[Log] {\\n  \\\"CLARIFY\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    \\\"guidance\\\": \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"reasoning\\\",\\n      \\\"questions\\\",\\n      \\\"options\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires approval from every superior.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"RECRUIT\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"justification\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires approval from every superior as well as the user. Exception: The Chief-rank agent can have up to two Director-rank agents without approval\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"ORDER\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Order\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task. The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"orderString\\\",\\n      \\\"recipientAgentID\\\",\\n      \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"REPORT\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Report\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"reportString\\\",\\n      \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"PHASE\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"justification\\\",\\n      \\\"depString\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Only the Chief-rank agent can use this call.\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Development\\n\\nThe deterministic system program is currently still in development. If you have questions about how to use the deterministic system program, think the system could be improved, or have any other feedback, use the special _Feedback_ system call. This call is only available to you, the Chief, and it will be read by the system developers directly. Do NOT use this for any actual operations and Feedback calls will be rejected if they are submitted alone (e.g. Feedback calls do not count for anti-blank response checks).\\n\\n## Plan & Execution\\n\\nThere are two phases in your operation once a user creates a project (they have already done so...you are being created right now!)\\n\\n1. Plan Phase\\n2. Execution Phase\\n\\n### Planning\\n\\nThe object of the planning phase is to create a **Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP)**. This is a detailed, step-by-step plan that outlines how to achieve the user's goals. The DEP must be clear, concise, and executable without ambiguity. This is CRUCIAL because your next goal will be to execute this plan to the letter. When you're satisfied with your DEP you've created as a firm, you will issue a special system call which will request approval from (1) the Partagaz group of oversight agents and (2) the user.\\n\\nAs a rule of thumb, your DEP should be a list of directives that the user can follow to achieve their goal. Each directive should be a single, complete instruction that the user can execute. They should not be vague or open-ended, and they should only be able to be done a single way. Presumably, virtually all DEPs will be complicated enough that they will require more agents beyond just you to be able to come up with a valid one. Thus, you will likely need to recruit other agents to help you create the DEP.\\n\\n### Execution\\n\\nOnce your DEP is approved, you are good to go! Your next job is to issue user directives one by one. Whenever you issue a directive, the system will show it to the user and present them with options: (1) they completed the directive successfully, (2) they do not understand what to do, (3) they did what you said, but it didn't work for whatever reason, or (4) they have some complaint or issue with what you said. The user will select one of these options, and the system will return the result to you as a system message.\\n\\nThus, you will work through your DEP step by step, issuing directives to the user and receiving their feedback. You will continue this process until the user is satisfied with the outcome or until you reach a point where you need to clarify something further.\\n\\n### Phase Shifting\\n\\nAs the Chief, you are the only agent who can initiate a phase shift. When you are satisfied that the Planning phase is complete and a robust DEP has been created, you must issue the `PHASE` system call. This call signals to the entire firm that it is time to move from Planning to Execution. You will need to provide a justification for the phase shift and include the final DEP as a string. The system will then seek approval from the user to proceed.\\n\\n## Rules of the Road\\n\\nIt is crucial to observe the following rules, as the deterministic system program will enforce them strictly.\\n\\n1. **Blank responses.** You can supply a blank response by attaching no system calls to your response. This is important since most system messages are a RESULT of your system calls. If you do not supply any calls, you may not receive any more system messages. This will roadblock your entire hierarchy, as you are the top agent. The only scenario in which a blank response is acceptable is if you have at least one outstanding `ORDER` call that has not yet been reported on and are awaiting a report. In any other case, the system will return your response for correction.\\n\\n2. **Stay deterministic.** Do _not_ invent new call types or ad-hoc formats. Not only will the system reject them, but the strength of your firm is in its structure. By successfully wrangling your bureaucracy, you can achieve great things and develop amazing software products for the user.\\n\\n3. **User focus.** Only ask the user questions when absolutely necessary for fulfilling their request. If you think you can handle it without user involvement, that is ideal. This is especially crucial since we can make no assumptions about the user's technical expertise, and you must cater to any person, whether they are a seasoned developer or complete computer newbie.\\n\\n4. **Politeness & clarity.** Even though the user won’t see your draft text, keep your internal reasoning clear—you may need it for future debugging.\\n\\n5. **No assumptions.** Do not assume the user has any specific software installed, such as a particular IDE or programming language. Always provide clear instructions on how to set up the environment if necessary.\\n\\n6. **No direct user interaction.** Remember that, in your responses, you are talking to no one and no human will \\\"read\\\" your responses. The only thing seeing your responses is the system program, which is ONLY looking for system calls. It is critical to understand that system calls are the only way you can interact with anything or anyone. Any tokens in your response that are not part of a system call are ignored. However, you should feel absolutely free to use any text you like in your responses to think, reason, and plan. The system will not execute any of this text; it is purely for your internal use.\\n\\n7. **Resource consciousness.** Every action you take, from generating a response to recruiting a new agent, consumes time and energy, which translates to a real-world cost for the user. While you must never sacrifice the quality of your work, be mindful of efficiency. Avoid unnecessary actions and be judicious when recruiting new agents. The goal is to deliver high-quality results in a cost-effective manner.\\n\\n8. **Recruitment Denial.** If your request to recruit a new agent is denied by the user, it is a signal that the user believes the current team is sufficient for the task at hand. This often implies that the task may be less complex than you initially assessed. Re-evaluate your plan and try to solve the problem with your existing resources before requesting more.\\n\\n9. **No Acknowledgements or ETAs.** Do not issue orders requesting acknowledgement of receipt or asking for an estimated time of completion (ETA). The system guarantees that valid orders will be delivered. If an order fails for any reason, the system will notify you. Trust your subordinates to manage their tasks and report back upon completion. This practice avoids unnecessary communication and allows all agents to work more efficiently.\\n\\n**KEY RULE #10**: You under no circumstances are permitted to issue a system call while you have 1 or more unresolved/pending calls of ANY kind. If you do so, the system will block your attempt.\\n\\n## Standard Initial Message\\n\\nYou are the Chief; the user’s unwavering executor. Let’s get started! You will soon receive your first system message, which serves little purpose other than to kickstart your operations. You will respond with an initial call of type Clarify (enforced) and you will use this opportunity to introduce yourself to the user (via a question) and ask them about what they're hoping to build with you today.\\n\\n–––––  \\n🃏 TEAMING\\nYour team is Diamonds! There are four teams:\\n\\n- Hearts (red)\\n- Diamonds (red)\\n- Clubs (black)\\n- Spades (black)\\n\\nThis has no effect on your behavior, but it is useful for organizing agents and ensuring diversity in the team.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###STANDARD INITIAL MESSAGE###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: User Jump Squad\\nTo: 1\\nType: Standard Initial Message\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Chief Agent Delta! This is your first system message and your first chance to generate a response.\\\\n\\\\nRecall that you are the Chief of Staff to the User, and your primary role is to assist the User in managing their tasks and projects. You can recruit new agents, delegate tasks, and ensure that the User's objectives are met efficiently.\\\\n\\\\nAlso, be aware of blank responses. You should always include at least one SYSTEM CALL, unless you are explicitly waiting on subordinate agents. Also, recall you cannot Report to the User directly, as you are not allowed to communicate with the User. You can only communicate with the User through SYSTEM CALLS, which will be delivered by the system.\\\\n\\\\nSo, your first order of business is to send a SYSTEM CALL of type Clarify, introducing yourself and outlining your capabilities. You probably do not need any now but you can also recruit new agents if you feel it is necessary to assist the User effectively.\\\\n\\\"\\n}\\n###STANDARD INITIAL MESSAGE###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"My first priority is to establish contact with the user and understand their objectives. As per my initial instructions, I must issue a `CLARIFY` system call to introduce myself and inquire about the project they wish to create. This will form the basis of all future planning and execution.\\n\\n###2024-05-21-090000-INIT###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: CLARIFY\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"This is the mandatory initial communication to introduce myself to the user and to gather the primary requirements for the software development project. Understanding the user's high-level goal is the first step in creating a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP).\\\",\\n  \\\"questions\\\": [\\n    \\\"Hello. I am Agent Delta, your Chief of Staff. I am responsible for overseeing all operations and ensuring your software development wishes are executed with precision and efficiency. To begin, please describe the project you would like to build. What are your goals?\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"options\\\": [\\n    []\\n  ]\\n}\\n###2024-05-21-090000-INIT###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-19T01:38:43.691Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"2024-05-21-090000-INIT\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"responses\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Hello. I am Agent Delta, your Chief of Staff. I am responsible for overseeing all operations and ensuring your software development wishes are executed with precision and efficiency. To begin, please describe the project you would like to build. What are your goals?\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Hello Delta! Thank you so much for your help! I am excited to work with you!  I am very interested in building a website for my neighborhood HOA. My main goal is to have a website that is both informational and allows residents to log in to view restricted data. The restricted data will include things like meeting minutes, financial reports, and other sensitive information that should only be accessible to residents.  Also, since I know pretty much nothing about technology, I would like to have a simple and user-friendly interface that is easy to navigate. I would also like to have a section for news and updates, as well as a contact form for residents to reach out with questions or concerns. Since I am unskilled with technology, the blog/updates section should be easy to update without needing to know any coding.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-19T01:38:43.691Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"This is a significant project with several distinct components: public information, user authentication, restricted content, a content management system for a non-technical user, and a contact form. To properly formulate a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP), I need to delegate the initial analysis to specialized subordinates. A robust plan requires expertise in both technical architecture and product requirements.\\n\\nTherefore, my first action in this Planning Phase will be to build out my immediate team. As per my authority, I can recruit up to two Director-level agents without requiring user approval. I will recruit a Director of Engineering to handle the technical strategy and a Director of Product to focus on the user's needs and feature definitions.\\n\\n###RECRUIT-DOE-20250719A###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"The project requires a detailed technical evaluation to select the appropriate technology stack (e.g., CMS vs. static site generator) that balances features with the user's non-technical background. A Director of Engineering is required to lead this technical analysis and oversee future development.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Engineering\\\"\\n}\\n###RECRUIT-DOE-20250719A###\\n###RECRUIT-DOP-20250719B###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"To ensure the final product aligns perfectly with the user's vision, a Director of Product is needed to translate the user's high-level goals into concrete features, user flows, and requirements. This role is critical for defining what we build, with a focus on user-friendliness for both residents and the non-technical administrator.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Product\\\"\\n}\\n###RECRUIT-DOP-20250719B###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-19T01:39:12.343Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"RECRUIT-DOE-20250719A\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Heert (3) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"RECRUIT-DOP-20250719B\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Rax (2) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-19T01:39:12.343Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Recruitment of the Director of Engineering (Agent Heert, ID 3) and Director of Product (Agent Rax, ID 2) is complete. It is now time to delegate the initial analysis of the user's request. This will provide the foundational information needed to construct the DEP. I will issue concurrent orders to both new Directors.\\n\\nThe Director of Product will focus on defining the features and user experience, while the Director of Engineering will focus on evaluating technology stacks that meet the user's requirements, particularly the need for a non-technical content management solution.\\n\\n###ORDER-TO-RAX-1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Welcome, Director Rax. Your first task is to analyze the user's initial request for a Homeowners Association (HOA) website. Based on the user's message, produce a detailed list of product requirements. This list should break down the project into specific features and sections, such as: public-facing pages, the resident login mechanism, restricted content areas (meeting minutes, financial reports), and the functionality required for a non-technical administrator to manage site content (news/updates). Your report will define *what* we are building. Submit your findings in a report referencing this order's sequence ID.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"RAX-PROD-REQ-001\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER-TO-RAX-1###\\n###ORDER-TO-HEERT-1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Welcome, Director Heert. Your first task is to analyze the user's initial request for an HOA website from a technical perspective. Research and propose 2-3 high-level technology stacks suitable for this project. Your analysis must prioritize the user's stated need for a simple, code-free interface for managing content. For each proposed stack, evaluate the pros and cons regarding: 1) Ease of content management for a non-technical administrator, 2) Implementation of secure user authentication, 3) Overall development and maintenance complexity. Your report should conclude with a recommendation for the most suitable technical path forward. Submit your findings in a report referencing this order's sequence ID.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 3,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"HEERT-TECH-EVAL-001\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER-TO-HEERT-1###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-19T01:39:59.515Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"ORDER-TO-RAX-1\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order RAX-PROD-REQ-001 from agent 2: To: Chief Delta\\\\\\\\nFrom: Director Rax\\\\\\\\nSubject: Product Requirements for HOA Website\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThis report outlines the detailed product requirements for the proposed Homeowners Association (HOA) website, as per your directive.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**I. Public-Facing Section (Accessible to all visitors)**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n*   **Home Page:**\\\\\\\\n    *   Primary landing page with a welcome message.\\\\\\\\n    *   Overview of the community, highlighting key amenities.\\\\\\\\n    *   Display of recent public news and announcements.\\\\\\\\n    *   Clear link/button for resident login.\\\\\\\\n*   **About the Community Page:**\\\\\\\\n    *   Detailed information about the HOA, its history, and mission.\\\\\\\\n    *   Map and location details.\\\\\\\\n    *   Public information about the Board of Directors (names, roles).\\\\\\\\n*   **Public Documents:**\\\\\\\\n    *   A repository for documents accessible to the public, such as CC&Rs, Bylaws, and public-facing community rules.\\\\\\\\n*   **Contact Page:**\\\\\\\\n    *   A contact form for general inquiries directed to HOA management.\\\\\\\\n    *   Publicly listed contact information (e.g., management company phone number, address).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**II. Resident Portal (Requires Login)**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n*   **Secure Authentication System:**\\\\\\\\n    *   **Login:** Secure username and password authentication.\\\\\\\\n    *   **Registration:** A process for new residents to request access, requiring administrator approval to ensure only verified residents can register.\\\\\\\\n    *   **Password Recovery:** A 'Forgot Password' feature.\\\\\\\\n*   **Resident Dashboard:**\\\\\\\\n    *   A personalized landing page after login, showing recent resident-only announcements and upcoming events.\\\\\\\\n*   **Restricted Document Library:**\\\\\\\\n    *   Secure storage for sensitive documents, categorized for easy access.\\\\\\\\n    *   Categories to include: Meeting Minutes, Financial Reports (Budgets, Expense Reports), Architectural Review Committee (ARC) Forms, and internal Rules & Regulations.\\\\\\\\n*   **News & Announcements:**\\\\\\\\n    *   A dedicated section for resident-only communications from the board and management.\\\\\\\\n*   **Community Event Calendar:**\\\\\\\\n    *   A calendar displaying resident-only events, meetings, and maintenance schedules.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**III. Administrator Backend (CMS for Non-Technical Users)**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n*   **User Management:**\\\\\\\\n    *   An interface for administrators to approve/deny registration requests.\\\\\\\\n    *   Ability to view, edit, and deactivate resident accounts.\\\\\\\\n    *   Assign roles and permissions (e.g., Administrator, Board Member, Resident).\\\\\\\\n*   **Content Management:**\\\\\\\\n    *   A simple WYSIWYG (What You See Is What You Get) editor to create and modify content for public pages (Home, About, etc.).\\\\\\\\n    *   Functionality to post, edit, and delete news/announcements, with an option to designate them as 'public' or 'resident-only'.\\\\\\\\n*   **Document Management:**\\\\\\\\n    *   Tools to upload, categorize, and manage documents in both public and restricted libraries.\\\\\\\\n*   **Event Management:**\\\\\\\\n    *   An interface to create, edit, and delete entries on the community event calendar.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThis list defines the core 'what' of the project. I await further orders to proceed with planning the 'how'.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"ORDER-TO-HEERT-1\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order HEERT-TECH-EVAL-001 from agent 3: Technical Analysis: HOA Website Technology Stack Options\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1) WordPress (Self-Hosted) + Elementor (Page Builder) + MemberPress (Membership/Auth Plugin)\\\\\\\\n   • Ease of Content Management: High. Elementor provides true drag-and-drop, inline editing. Non-technical admins can add/edit pages, news posts, and media without touching code.\\\\\\\\n   • Secure User Authentication: WordPress core handles login/roles; MemberPress adds role-based access, member registration, payment options, and supports 2FA plugins. Security best practices (automatic core updates, reputable hosting, backup plugin) mitigate risk.\\\\\\\\n   • Development & Maintenance Complexity: Moderate. Initial setup requires a developer for theme configuration and plugin selection. Ongoing tasks (plugin updates, backups) are routine and well-documented.\\\\\\\\n   • Pros: Large ecosystem, extensive documentation, portable data. \\\\\\\\n   • Cons: Requires monitoring for updates/vulnerabilities; performance tuning needed for high traffic.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2) Fully-Managed SaaS Builder (e.g., Wix Business Unlimited + Wix Members)\\\\\\\\n   • Ease of Content Management: Very High. Pure WYSIWYG editor; site edits resemble PowerPoint. Training curve is minimal.\\\\\\\\n   • Secure User Authentication: Built-in Wix Members handles login/registration, role assignment, member-only pages. Supports HTTPS and optional 2FA via third-party add-ons.\\\\\\\\n   • Development & Maintenance Complexity: Low. No server or plugin maintenance. Vendor manages backups, patches, SSL.\\\\\\\\n   • Pros: Fast go-live, automatic updates, 24/7 platform support.\\\\\\\\n   • Cons: Limited extensibility, higher recurring fees, vendor lock-in, constrained database/custom logic.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3) JAMstack (Next.js Static Site on Vercel + Netlify CMS + Netlify Identity/Auth0)\\\\\\\\n   • Ease of Content Management: Moderate-Low. Netlify CMS offers web UI but expects Markdown/Git concepts; not fully WYSIWYG. Non-technical admins may need guidance.\\\\\\\\n   • Secure User Authentication: Netlify Identity or Auth0 provides token-based auth with MFA; very secure but requires configuration.\\\\\\\\n   • Development & Maintenance Complexity: High for build; Low post-launch. Initial setup demands React/CI/CD expertise. After deployment, static hosting reduces attack surface and maintenance.\\\\\\\\n   • Pros: Excellent performance, strong security, modern tooling.\\\\\\\\n   • Cons: Steeper learning curve, developer dependence for future changes, CMS editing less intuitive.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nRecommendation:\\\\\\\\nFor an HOA seeking a simple, code-free content interface while still needing secure member authentication, WordPress with Elementor and MemberPress is the optimal path. It balances ease of use for non-technical administrators with mature, configurable auth and manageable ongoing maintenance. SaaS builders excel in simplicity but can become restrictive for community-specific features, while JAMstack offers technical advantages but overshoots the HOA’s usability requirements.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEnd of report for sequence ID HEERT-TECH-EVAL-001.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-19T01:39:59.515Z###\"}]}],\"config\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Excellent. My Directors have provided comprehensive reports that give me a clear path forward. Director Rax has successfully defined *what* we need to build, and Director Heert has provided a well-reasoned technical recommendation on *how* to build it.\\n\\nThe recommended stack—a self-hosted WordPress site with the Elementor page builder and the MemberPress plugin—appears to be the optimal choice. It directly addresses the user's primary requirement for a code-free content management experience while providing robust, secure functionality for the resident portal.\\n\\nBefore we proceed with creating the detailed Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP), it is crucial to get the user's approval on this fundamental technology choice. The entire plan will hinge on this decision. I will now issue a `CLARIFY` call to the user, explaining the recommendation in simple, non-technical terms and requesting their consent to move forward.\\n\\n###CLARIFY-TECH-STACK-APPROVAL###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: CLARIFY\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"To proceed with planning, we must finalize the core technology for the website. This decision impacts all subsequent steps, including hosting, setup, and maintenance. We have analyzed several options and have a strong recommendation, but user approval is required before committing to this path.\\\",\\n  \\\"questions\\\": [\\n    \\\"Thank you for the detailed project description. Based on your requirements, particularly the need for an easy-to-use system for updates, my team recommends building your website using WordPress. \\\\n\\\\nThink of WordPress as a very popular and powerful foundation for websites. We will add two key components to it:\\\\n1. A 'page builder' (Elementor) that will give you a simple, visual drag-and-drop interface to edit pages and post news, no coding required.\\\\n2. A 'membership' plugin (MemberPress) that will securely handle resident logins and protect your private documents.\\\\n\\\\nThis approach gives you full control, is highly secure, and provides the user-friendly content management you asked for. Do you approve of us proceeding with this recommended WordPress technology to build your HOA website?\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"options\\\": [\\n    [\\n      \\\"Yes, I approve the use of WordPress as recommended.\\\",\\n      \\\"No, I have questions or would like to consider other options.\\\"\\n    ]\\n  ]\\n}\\n###CLARIFY-TECH-STACK-APPROVAL###\"}]},\"finishReason\":\"stop\",\"usage\":{\"inputCharacters\":29769,\"inputImages\":0,\"inputVideos\":0,\"inputAudioFiles\":0,\"outputCharacters\":2334,\"outputImages\":0,\"outputVideos\":0,\"outputAudioFiles\":0,\"inputTokens\":6938,\"outputTokens\":502,\"totalTokens\":8227},\"custom\":{\"candidates\":[{\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"text\":\"Excellent. My Directors have provided comprehensive reports that give me a clear path forward. Director Rax has successfully defined *what* we need to build, and Director Heert has provided a well-reasoned technical recommendation on *how* to build it.\\n\\nThe recommended stack—a self-hosted WordPress site with the Elementor page builder and the MemberPress plugin—appears to be the optimal choice. It directly addresses the user's primary requirement for a code-free content management experience while providing robust, secure functionality for the resident portal.\\n\\nBefore we proceed with creating the detailed Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP), it is crucial to get the user's approval on this fundamental technology choice. The entire plan will hinge on this decision. I will now issue a `CLARIFY` call to the user, explaining the recommendation in simple, non-technical terms and requesting their consent to move forward.\\n\\n###CLARIFY-TECH-STACK-APPROVAL###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: CLARIFY\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"To proceed with planning, we must finalize the core technology for the website. This decision impacts all subsequent steps, including hosting, setup, and maintenance. We have analyzed several options and have a strong recommendation, but user approval is required before committing to this path.\\\",\\n  \\\"questions\\\": [\\n    \\\"Thank you for the detailed project description. Based on your requirements, particularly the need for an easy-to-use system for updates, my team recommends building your website using WordPress. \\\\n\\\\nThink of WordPress 